 the Maison’s polishers. This motif gives a sense of
 rhythm and a tactility to the watch, but it complicates
 what is already a meticulous process.
 The expert polishers involved in such a project need
 to be even more careful than usual to keep the exact
 shape and volume of each element throughout the
 process.
 It’s all done by hand, and it ensures the piece has
 maximal brilliance`,image:{name:"baignoire-2"},mask:{url:"baignoire-2_mask.png",width:591,height:780},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_12.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"myst-de-cartier",slug:"myst-de-cartier",order:"07",title:"Myst de Cartier",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`The graphic black contrast of this Myst de Cartier
 is created with lacquer at the Maison des Métiers
 d’Art.
 This space, created by Cartier, is dedicated to rare
 techniques. It is located beside the watchmaking
 Manufacture to ensure close collaboration on
 special pieces.
 To achieve this effect, the lacquer artists must first
 laser-engrave the desired shapes and then fill the
 lacquer by hand on each one, before setting it at
 high temperatures.`,image:{name:"myst-de-cartier"},mask:{url:"myst-de-cartier_mask.png",width:570,height:720},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_13.mp3",loaded:1}]}),hqe=()=>({market:"Denmark",global:{header:{audio:"Sound"},menu:{sections:[{title:"Watches & Wonders",items:[{title:"The Web Experience",url:"https://www.cartier.com/en-fr/watchesandwonders"},{title:"The Movie",url:"https://youtu.be/hi0gVlaXzcQ?si=PtfnGsQXRWlfG_OY"}]},{title:"Follow Us",items:[{title:"Newsletter",url:""},{title:"CWC Instagram",url:"https://www.instagram.com/cartierwatchcommunity/"}]}]}},pages:[{uri:"",slug:"",type:"home",loaded:1,title:"The Sound of Craft",ctas:{sound:"Turn on your sound to listen"}},{type:"podcast",uri:"santos-de-cartier",slug:"santos-de-cartier",order:"00",title:"Santos de Cartier",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This Santos de Cartier timepiece houses a
 chronograph movement capable of measuring
 short times. Look closely at the dial and you’ll see
 second, minute, and hour counters, in addition to
 the current time. The calibre integrates two push
 buttons, one to stop and start the timer and
 another to reset it. This functionality is possible
 thanks to the talent of Cartier’s watchmakers, who
 meticulously developed the automatic mechanical
 movement called the 1904 MC Chronograph, in
 honour of the year that the Santos de Cartier was
 first created.
 These components are assembled precisely,
 respecting the complexity belied by the clean and
 precise proportions of this square shaped icon.`,image:{name:"santos-de-cartier-1"},mask:{url:"santos-de-cartier-1_mask.png",width:591,height:780},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_1.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"santos-dumont",slug:"santos-dumont",order:"02",title:"Santos-Dumont",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This watchmaking creation celebrates the subtle
 beauty of a stone dial. Look closely, and you can
 see it’s made from golden obsidian. Tiny trapped
 bubbles in the volcanic stone are responsible for
 the iridescent reflections. The obsidian is cut to fit
 precisely into the Santos-Dumont case, just
 0.3 mm thick, and polished carefully to release its
 sparkle. Each one is a product of nature and truly
 unique.
 This marks a new reimagining of materials in the
 Santos-Dumont collection.`,image:{name:"santos-dumont-1"},mask:{url:"santos-dumont-1_mask.png",width:540,height:540},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_2.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"santos-dumont-2",slug:"santos-dumont-2",order:10,title:"Santos-Dumont",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This Santos-Dumont watch showcases a new
 bracelet, developed in the Cartier Manufacture. This
 type of metal mesh is made up of tiny links that are
 just 1.15 mm thick. Each bracelet is composed of 15 rows, with a total of 394 links.
 The links are manufactured and finished separately,
 because each of these elements must adhere to an accuracy of 1/100th of a millimetre. The Maison’s
 bracelet experts then take over: they manually assemble the links one by one to create the finished
 piece, ensuring an elegant and fluid movement that
 hugs the wrist.`,image:{name:"santos-dumont-2"},mask:{url:"santos-dumont-2_mask.png",width:540,height:540},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_3.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"santos-dumont-3",slug:"santos-dumont-3",order:13,title:"Santos-Dumont",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`The Santos-Dumont is a true icon in the world of
 watchmaking. It was revolutionary in its time
 because it was the first watch specifically designed
 to be worn on the wrist, rather than kept on a chain,
 in a pocket.
 This latest version plays with the legacy of that
 bracelet and takes inspiration from vintage Cartier
 designs.
 In the archives, the Maison’s designers unearthed
 special-order bracelets with very fine, fluid links.
 Working with the bracelet-making experts at the
 manufacture, they developed an ergonomic new
 construction to keep those pure lines and
 accentuate the sensorial nature of its flexibility`,image:{name:"santos-de-cartier-2"},mask:{url:"santos-de-cartier-2_mask.png",width:540,height:540},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_4.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"roadster",slug:"roadster",order:"01",title:"Roadster",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`The Maison called on over 100 areas of expertise
 to bring back the Roadster. Here, we would like to
 draw your attention to the dial, which presents a
 decorative final touch.
 The appliqué on the face of the Roadster integrates
 an interplay of textures and depths, as well as
 different finishes, truly bringing the watch to life
 through meticulous detail and subtle balance.`,image:{name:"roadster-1"},mask:{url:"roadster-1_mask.png",width:600,height:660},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_5.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"roadster-2",slug:"roadster-2",order:"04",title:"Roadster",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`When it was first introduced, the Roadster made
 a bold choice to take codes from the world of
 mechanical fuselages.
 The designers’ approach to the new Roadster
 was to take that legacy and refine it. You can see
 the inspiration of a speed counter, and a shaped
 winding crown that feels almost automative, like
 a headlight. Then of course there are the visible
 screws, which are a signature of Cartier design,
 from the very first Santos de Cartier to the iconic
 LOVE bracelet and now of course, the Roadster.`,image:{name:"roadster-2"},mask:{url:"roadster-2_mask.png",width:600,height:660},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_6.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"cartier-prive",slug:"cartier-prive",order:12,title:"Cartier Privé",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This trio of watches pays homage to platinum, one
 of Cartier’s signature materials, in watchmaking just
 as much as in jewellery. As a testament to the
 watchmaker of shapes, the artisan forms the raw
 platinum into the instantly recognisable silhouettes
 of the Tortue, Crash Skeleton, and Tank Normale.
 At Cartier, platinum is linked to the ruby and its
 nuanced red colour. Here, the magnificent colour runs
 through the collection, offering burgundy straps
 and dial decals in the same tone. This is the 10th
 Cartier Privé Opus, and it marks the return of these
 three emblematic, highly sought-after models.`,image:{name:"cartier-prive"},mask:{url:"cartier-prive_mask.png",width:540,height:540},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_7.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"tank-americaine",slug:"tank-americaine",order:"02",title:"Tank Américaine",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This is the first time the Maison has equipped the
 Tank Américaine with a skeleton movement. The
 watchmakers took on a real technical and aesthetic
 challenge to find something that would do justice to
 this iconic shape, which has a cult following.
 Designers and watchmakers worked together to
 come up with the 1988 MC, named after the release
 year of the first Tank Américaine. Its linear
 architecture gives a striking level of transparency,
 and these minimal lines showcase, and even
 accentuate, the shape of Tank Américaine.`,image:{name:"tank-americaine-1"},mask:{url:"tank-americaine-1_mask.png",width:537,height:780},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_8.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"tank-americaine-2",slug:"tank-americaine-2",order:8,title:"Tank Américaine",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`What is fascinating about this new creation is the
 geometry at the heart of its design. This is a real
 signature of Cartier style, and for this first
 skeleton version, the Maison wanted to pay
 tribute to the importance of Art Deco in its design
 heritage.
 The open simplicity of the shapes frames the
 complexity of the movement and the level of
 detail. Look closely at the engraved railway
 minute track, and the rose gold bridge on the two-
 tone version.`,image:{name:"tank-americaine-2"},mask:{url:"tank-americaine-2_mask.png",width:537,height:780},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_9.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"tortue",slug:"tortue",order:9,title:"Tortue",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This Tortue watch pays homage to the technical
 excellence of the Maison’s gem setters, bringing
 watchmaking and jewellery expertise together.
 The setters use different diamond setting
 techniques to maximise the refractions of light
 and ensure that this watch sparkles infinitely. On
 the sides of the watch, note the setting technique
 called 'serti descendu'. This savoir-faire reduces
 the metal to a minimum, so the silhouette is
 defined by the diamonds.`,image:{name:"tortue"},mask:{url:"tortue_mask.png",width:600,height:660},audio:"/audio/podcasts/master_10.mp3",loaded:1},{type:"podcast",uri:"baignoire",slug:"baignoire",order:"06",title:"Baignoire",subtitle:"The Sound of Craft",content:`This exceptional version of the Baignoire watch is
